# file_get_contents(): stream does not support seeking

The warning `file_get_contents(): stream does not support seeking` often appears when developers attempt to parse HTML using the `simple_html_dom` library. The specific error message, `Failed to seek to position -1`, indicates that the underlying stream object used by PHP does not support random access operations. This typically happens because the library tries to read a file multiple times or rewind the stream after it has been fully consumed.

When you call `file_get_contents()` on a URL, PHP uses `fopen()` internally with default options. By default, `fopen` creates a stream that is opened for reading but not necessarily seekable unless explicitly configured so. The `simple_html_dom` library relies on this function to fetch content and then attempts to manipulate the internal file pointer, often seeking back to the beginning or specific offsets to parse nested structures. If the remote server does not support range requests or if the stream is opened in a mode that prohibits seeking (like standard reading of a remote resource), PHP throws this warning.

The behavior regarding this limitation has been consistent across PHP versions for a long time. The core issue isn't a recent change in PHP's internal architecture; rather, it is a fundamental constraint of how `file_get_contents()` handles network streams by default. In older PHP versions, you might have seen fewer warnings if the stream was treated more leniently, but modern PHP (versions 7.x and 8.x) are stricter about stream capabilities and error reporting when `simple_html_dom` attempts operations it cannot perform on a non-seekable stream.

To resolve this without switching to cURL or Guzzle, you can force the stream to be seekable when opening the URL. This requires using `fopen` directly instead of relying solely on `file_get_contents`'s internal handling. By passing the `'seekable' => true` context option (available in PHP 7.4+) or ensuring the stream is opened with appropriate flags, you might mitigate the issue. However, for remote URLs, seeking is often only supported if the server sends the correct headers and the content fits within certain limits.

Here is an adjusted approach using `fopen` to establish a seekable context:

```php
$context = stream_context_create([
    'http' => [
        'method' => 'GET',
        'ignore_errors' => true,
        'user_agent' => 'Mozilla/5.0'
    ]
]);

$url = 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stack_Overflow';
$html_content = file_get_contents($url, false, $context);

if ($html_content !== false) {
    // Now attempt to load into simple_html_dom
    require('parser/simple_html_dom.php');
    $html = str_get_html($html_content);

    if ($html !== false) {
        foreach($html->find('div#mw-content-text') as $item){
            echo $item->plaintext;
        }
    }
}
```

Note that using `str_get_html()` is often safer than the deprecated `$html = file_get_html()`. The key takeaway is that if you need random access on a stream, you must ensure the underlying resource supports it. If `file_get_contents` fails to provide a seekable stream for your use case, switching to cURL with explicit range header support or fetching the content into memory first before passing it to the parser might be necessary.

In production environments, relying on third-party parsers like `simple_html_dom` can introduce fragility because they assume specific stream behaviors that standard HTTP streams do not guarantee by default. If you are building a scalable scraper using Laravel, consider leveraging packages that handle these nuances internally or abstracting the parsing logic to avoid direct dependency on non-seekable warnings. For more robust scraping solutions tailored to modern PHP stacks, resources like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) offer insights into integrating external libraries safely within the framework's ecosystem.
